const QUESTIONS = [{"cat":"Testicle Disorders","q":"Testicular torsion is usually due to congenital failure of the testis to attach to the scrotal lining via the:","opts":["Tunica albuginea","Vas deferens","Gubernaculum","Processus vaginalis"],"a":3,"exp":"An abnormally mobile testis due to a congenital defect in processus vaginalis attachment predisposes to torsion, presenting with sudden pain and absent cremasteric reflex.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Nephritic Syndrome","q":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis typically occurs how long after the preceding infection?","opts":["2-3 weeks","Immediately, during the infection","3 months","1-2 days"],"a":0,"exp":"PSGN classically presents 2-3 weeks after group A streptococcal infection of the skin or pharynx.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Nephrolithiasis","q":"Cystine stones result from a genetic defect causing decreased tubular reabsorption of:","opts":["Cysteine","Urate","Phosphate","Calcium"],"a":0,"exp":"Cystinuria impairs tubular reabsorption of cysteine, leading to cystine stone formation.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Chronic Renal Failure","q":"Hypocalcemia in chronic renal failure occurs because of decreased renal:","opts":["Reabsorption of calcium in the loop of Henle","1-alpha-hydroxylation of vitamin D","Calcitonin secretion","Parathyroid hormone secretion"],"a":1,"exp":"Failing proximal tubule cells cannot adequately 1-alpha-hydroxylate vitamin D, lowering calcium.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Bronchiectasis","q":"Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis, a cause of bronchiectasis, is a hypersensitivity reaction to Aspergillus most often seen in patients with:","opts":["Asthma or cystic fibrosis","Primary pulmonary hypertension","Silicosis","Sarcoidosis"],"a":0,"exp":"ABPA causes chronic inflammatory airway damage through hypersensitivity to Aspergillus, typically in the setting of preexisting asthma or CF.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease","q":"Grossly, ulcerative colitis shows pseudopolyps and loss of haustra, producing which imaging sign?","opts":["'Double bubble' sign","'Bird-beak' sign","'Lead pipe' sign","'String' sign"],"a":2,"exp":"Chronic UC leads to a featureless, tubular colon with loss of haustral markings, described as the 'lead pipe' sign.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Testicular Germ Cell Tumors","q":"The most common testicular tumor in children is:","opts":["Choriocarcinoma","Leydig cell tumor","Yolk sac (endodermal sinus) tumor","Seminoma"],"a":2,"exp":"Yolk sac tumor is the predominant testicular tumor in the pediatric population, unlike in adults where seminoma predominates.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Testicular Germ Cell Tumors","q":"Yolk sac tumor is histologically characterized by glomerulus-like structures known as:","opts":["Reinke crystals","Schiller-Duval bodies","Call-Exner bodies","Psammoma bodies"],"a":1,"exp":"Schiller-Duval bodies, resembling primitive glomeruli, are the classic histologic finding in yolk sac tumor.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Prostatitis & BPH","q":"The prostate gland lies at the base of the bladder, encircling the urethra, and its posterior surface is palpable on:","opts":["Digital rectal examination","Abdominal palpation","Scrotal examination","Transurethral cystoscopy only"],"a":0,"exp":"The prostate sits anterior to the rectum, allowing its posterior aspect to be palpated during a digital rectal exam.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Nephrotic Syndrome","q":"The characteristic electron microscopy finding in minimal change disease is:","opts":["Spike and dome deposits","Tram-track appearance","Subepithelial humps","Effacement of foot processes"],"a":3,"exp":"Effacement of foot processes on EM is the hallmark of MCD, with normal-appearing glomeruli on light microscopy.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Stomach","q":"The diffuse type of gastric carcinoma is characterized by signet ring cells and desmoplasia causing thickening of the stomach wall, known as:","opts":["Napkin-ring lesion","Linitis plastica","Lead-pipe appearance","Cobblestone mucosa"],"a":1,"exp":"Diffuse-type gastric carcinoma diffusely infiltrates the wall with signet ring cells, producing a rigid 'linitis plastica' stomach; it is not associated with H. pylori.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Pleura","q":"Tension pneumothorax, arising from penetrating chest wall injury, is a medical emergency in which air enters the pleural space but cannot exit, causing the trachea to shift:","opts":["Not at all","Downward toward the diaphragm","Toward the side of injury","Away from the side of injury"],"a":3,"exp":"In tension pneumothorax, progressively accumulating trapped air pushes mediastinal structures, including the trachea, away from the affected side.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Testicular Germ Cell Tumors","q":"Testicular teratoma may be associated with elevation of which tumor markers?","opts":["PSA only","CEA only","Calcitonin only","AFP or beta-hCG"],"a":3,"exp":"Teratomas, especially when mixed with other germ cell elements, may show elevated AFP or beta-hCG.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Prostate Adenocarcinoma","q":"In Gleason grading, scores from two distinct tumor areas (each rated 1-5) are added together to produce a total score ranging from:","opts":["2 to 10","1 to 5","1 to 4","0 to 100"],"a":0,"exp":"The Gleason score sums the grades of the two most prevalent tumor patterns, yielding a total score between 2 and 10, with higher scores indicating worse prognosis.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease","q":"Ulcerative colitis involves inflammation limited to which layers of the bowel wall?","opts":["Serosa only","Muscularis propria only","Mucosa and submucosa","Full thickness of the bowel wall"],"a":2,"exp":"Unlike Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis is limited to the mucosal and submucosal layers.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"COPD","q":"Chronic bronchitis is characterized histologically by hypertrophy of bronchial mucous glands, quantified by an increased:","opts":["FEV1:FVC ratio","Reid index (greater than 50%)","Alveolar-arterial gradient","A1AT level"],"a":1,"exp":"The Reid index measures gland layer thickness relative to bronchial wall thickness; it exceeds 50% in chronic bronchitis compared to a normal value under 40%.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Bronchiectasis","q":"Bronchiectasis is defined as permanent dilation of the bronchi due to severe inflammation and damage to airway walls, resulting in:","opts":["Alveolar wall destruction only","Loss of airway tone with air trapping","Formation of hyaline membranes","Increased airway tone with restriction"],"a":1,"exp":"Chronic inflammatory damage weakens bronchial walls, causing permanent dilation, loss of tone, and air trapping.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Prostatitis & BPH","q":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia characteristically occurs in which zone of the prostate?","opts":["Peripheral posterior zone","Transitional apex","Central periurethral zone","Anterior fibromuscular zone"],"a":2,"exp":"BPH arises in the central periurethral zone, in contrast to prostate adenocarcinoma, which typically arises peripherally.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Vagina","q":"The vaginal mucosa is lined by:","opts":["Columnar epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Keratinizing squamous epithelium","Nonkeratinizing squamous epithelium"],"a":3,"exp":"The vaginal canal is lined by nonkeratinizing squamous epithelium.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease","q":"Crohn disease grossly demonstrates cobblestone mucosa, creeping fat, and strictures, producing which finding on imaging?","opts":["'Lead pipe' sign","'Double bubble' sign","'Napkin-ring' sign","'String' sign"],"a":3,"exp":"Strictures from transmural Crohn disease inflammation create a narrowed lumen visible as the 'string sign' on imaging.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Diabetes Mellitus","q":"Type 2 diabetes mellitus, accounting for about 90% of cases, typically arises in obese, middle-aged adults due to:","opts":["Decreased numbers of insulin receptors causing end-organ resistance","A congenital enzyme defect in insulin synthesis","Autoimmune destruction of beta cells","Ectopic insulin-secreting tumor"],"a":0,"exp":"Obesity reduces insulin receptor numbers, causing insulin resistance that characterizes type 2 diabetes, which also has a strong genetic component.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Renal Neoplasia","q":"Renal angiomyolipoma occurs with increased frequency in patients with:","opts":["WAGR syndrome","Von Hippel-Lindau disease","Tuberous sclerosis","Neurofibromatosis type 1"],"a":2,"exp":"Angiomyolipomas are seen with increased frequency in tuberous sclerosis.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Tuberculosis","q":"Primary tuberculosis results in focal caseating necrosis of the lower lobe and hilar lymph nodes, which undergoes fibrosis and calcification to form a:","opts":["Ghon complex","Cavitary lesion","Pancoast lesion","Miliary lesion"],"a":0,"exp":"The Ghon complex represents the healed combination of the primary lung lesion and involved hilar lymph node.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Renal Neoplasia","q":"Angiomyolipoma is composed of blood vessels, smooth muscle, and:","opts":["Adipose tissue","Lymphoid tissue","Bone","Cartilage"],"a":0,"exp":"Angiomyolipoma is a hamartoma of blood vessels, smooth muscle, and adipose tissue.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Parathyroid","q":"Parathyroid hormone increases serum calcium through increased bone resorption, increased renal calcium reabsorption, and:","opts":["Increased calcitonin release","Increased renal phosphate reabsorption","Increased intestinal calcium absorption via activation of vitamin D","Direct inhibition of osteoclasts"],"a":2,"exp":"PTH indirectly increases intestinal calcium absorption by activating vitamin D, in addition to its direct effects on bone and kidney.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Testicular Germ Cell Tumors","q":"Testicular tumors classically present as a firm, painless testicular mass that, unlike a hydrocele, cannot be:","opts":["Palpated on exam","Detected by ultrasound","Associated with elevated tumor markers","Transilluminated"],"a":3,"exp":"A solid testicular tumor does not transilluminate, distinguishing it from a fluid-filled hydrocele.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Endometrium & Myometrium","q":"Endometrial carcinoma is the most common invasive carcinoma of the female genital tract and, in its hyperplasia pathway (75% of cases), shows histology described as:","opts":["Squamous","Clear cell","Endometrioid","Serous"],"a":2,"exp":"The hyperplasia pathway of endometrial carcinoma produces endometrioid histology, resembling normal endometrial glands, typically at around age 60.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Colonic Polyps & Polyposis Syndromes","q":"The most common type of colonic polyp, which shows a serrated appearance on microscopy and has no malignant potential, is the:","opts":["Adenomatous polyp","Hyperplastic polyp","Peutz-Jeghers polyp","Juvenile polyp"],"a":1,"exp":"Hyperplastic polyps are the most common colonic polyp, showing a serrated pattern from glandular hyperplasia, and are benign with no malignant potential.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Prostatitis & BPH","q":"5\u03b1-reductase inhibitors treat BPH by blocking the conversion of testosterone to DHT, but they take a relatively long time to work because:","opts":["They require concurrent use with an \u03b11-antagonist to have any effect","They act over months, requiring gradual shrinkage of hyperplastic tissue","They must first be converted to an active metabolite in the liver","They only work after prostatectomy"],"a":1,"exp":"5\u03b1-reductase inhibitors produce gradual, months-long improvement as hyperplastic prostatic tissue slowly regresses.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Chronic Renal Failure","q":"Uremia in chronic renal failure can result in encephalopathy characterized by:","opts":["Chorea","Ataxia","Asterixis","Resting tremor"],"a":2,"exp":"Uremic encephalopathy is classically characterized by asterixis.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Colon Disorders","q":"Hirschsprung disease results from congenital failure of neural crest-derived ganglion cells to descend into the myenteric and submucosal plexus, and is associated with:","opts":["Klinefelter syndrome","Down syndrome","Marfan syndrome","Turner syndrome"],"a":1,"exp":"Hirschsprung disease is associated with Down syndrome and causes defective relaxation and peristalsis of the distal colon.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Vulva","q":"Lichen simplex chronicus, presenting as thick, leathery leukoplakia from chronic irritation and scratching, is characterized histologically by:","opts":["Koilocytic change","Malignant epithelial cells within the epidermis","Hyperplasia of the vulvar squamous epithelium","Thinning of the epidermis with dermal fibrosis"],"a":2,"exp":"Unlike lichen sclerosus (epidermal thinning), lichen simplex chronicus shows squamous epithelial hyperplasia and carries no increased cancer risk.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Chronic Renal Failure","q":"Renal osteodystrophy in chronic renal failure develops secondary to:","opts":["Primary hyperthyroidism","Vitamin D toxicity","Hypercalcemia","Secondary hyperparathyroidism"],"a":3,"exp":"Renal osteodystrophy results from secondary hyperparathyroidism along with osteomalacia and osteoporosis.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Prostate Adenocarcinoma","q":"Definitive diagnosis of prostate adenocarcinoma requires:","opts":["Prostatic biopsy","Digital rectal exam alone","PSA level alone","Transrectal ultrasound alone"],"a":0,"exp":"While PSA and DRE can raise suspicion, a tissue biopsy is required to confirm the diagnosis of prostate adenocarcinoma.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Urinary Tract Infection","q":"Chronic pyelonephritis in children is most often due to:","opts":["Vesicoureteral reflux","Diabetes mellitus","Analgesic abuse","Nephrolithiasis"],"a":0,"exp":"Vesicoureteral reflux is the leading cause of chronic pyelonephritis in children, leading to recurrent ascending infection.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Diabetes Mellitus","q":"Diabetic ketoacidosis is often precipitated by physiologic stress, during which epinephrine stimulates glucagon secretion, increasing lipolysis and production of:","opts":["Amyloid deposits in the islets","Glycated hemoglobin","Sorbitol via aldose reductase","Free fatty acids converted to ketone bodies by the liver"],"a":3,"exp":"Stress-induced glucagon and catecholamine surges increase lipolysis; the liver converts the resulting free fatty acids into ketone bodies, causing DKA.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Small Bowel","q":"Duodenal atresia, associated with Down syndrome, produces which classic imaging finding?","opts":["'Double bubble' sign","'Bird-beak' sign","'Lead pipe' sign","'String' sign"],"a":0,"exp":"Duodenal atresia causes distension of the stomach and proximal duodenum, producing the 'double bubble' sign along with polyhydramnios and bilious vomiting.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Pleura","q":"Mesothelioma classically presents with recurrent pleural effusions, dyspnea, chest pain, and a tumor that:","opts":["Produces PTHrP-mediated hypercalcemia","Metastasizes early to bone","Encases the lung","Forms a solitary peripheral nodule"],"a":2,"exp":"Mesothelioma spreads along the pleural surface, encasing the lung and producing recurrent effusions rather than forming a discrete mass.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Cushing Syndrome","q":"Cushing disease, an ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma, accounts for the majority of endogenous Cushing syndrome cases and is distinguished from ectopic ACTH secretion by:","opts":["Suppression of cortisol with high-dose dexamethasone","Complete absence of ACTH elevation","Presence of hyperkalemia","Low renin and low aldosterone"],"a":0,"exp":"Pituitary ACTH-secreting adenomas typically remain partially responsive to high-dose dexamethasone, unlike ectopic ACTH-secreting tumors, which show no suppression.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Tuberculosis","q":"Biopsy in tuberculosis reveals caseating granulomas, and organisms are identified using which special stain?","opts":["Congo red stain","Acid-fast bacillus (AFB) stain","Silver stain","PAS stain"],"a":1,"exp":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis is identified histologically using an acid-fast stain due to its unique mycolic acid-containing cell wall.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Nephrolithiasis","q":"The most common underlying cause of calcium-containing kidney stones is:","opts":["Cystinuria","Idiopathic hypercalciuria","Chronic UTI","Gout"],"a":1,"exp":"Idiopathic hypercalciuria is the most common cause of calcium stones.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Endometrium & Myometrium","q":"A diagnosis of chronic endometritis requires the presence of which cell type, since lymphocytes are normally found in the endometrium?","opts":["Plasma cells","Eosinophils","Neutrophils","Multinucleated giant cells"],"a":0,"exp":"Because lymphocytes are a normal endometrial finding, plasma cells are required to diagnose chronic endometritis.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Acute Renal Failure","q":"With long-standing urinary tract obstruction, tubular damage develops, resulting in which laboratory pattern?","opts":["BUN:Cr > 15 and FENa < 1%","No change in FENa","Urine osm persistently > 500","BUN:Cr < 15 and FENa > 2%"],"a":3,"exp":"Chronic obstruction damages tubules, so reabsorption of BUN and sodium falls, dropping BUN:Cr below 15 and raising FENa above 2%.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Lung Cancer","q":"Lung carcinoma is broadly divided into small cell carcinoma, which accounts for about 15% of cases, and:","opts":["Carcinoid tumor (about 85% of cases)","Bronchial hamartoma (about 85% of cases)","Mesothelioma (about 85% of cases)","Non-small cell carcinoma (about 85% of cases)"],"a":3,"exp":"Non-small cell carcinomas, comprising adenocarcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, and large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma, make up the majority of lung cancers.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Asthma","q":"Non-atopic (nonallergic) asthma can be triggered by exercise, viral infection, occupational exposures, and:","opts":["Dust mites","Pollen","Pet dander","Aspirin"],"a":3,"exp":"Unlike atopic asthma, non-atopic asthma is triggered by non-allergic stimuli, including aspirin in susceptible individuals.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Penis","q":"Bowenoid papulosis, an in situ carcinoma presenting as multiple reddish papules in younger patients, is distinguished from Bowen disease and erythroplasia of Queyrat by the fact that it:","opts":["Does not progress to invasive carcinoma","Always progresses to invasive carcinoma","Occurs exclusively in elderly patients","Is not associated with HPV"],"a":0,"exp":"Unlike Bowen disease and erythroplasia of Queyrat, bowenoid papulosis typically remains in situ and does not progress to invasive cancer.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Lung Cancer","q":"Small cell lung carcinoma is composed of poorly differentiated small cells with neuroendocrine differentiation, is chromogranin positive, and typically arises in a:","opts":["Peripheral location in female nonsmokers","Central location, unrelated to smoking","Peripheral location in male smokers","Central location in male smokers"],"a":3,"exp":"Small cell carcinoma is strongly linked to smoking and classically arises centrally, showing aggressive behavior with early metastasis.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Ovarian Tumors \u2013 Germ Cell","q":"Dysgerminoma, which generally carries a good prognosis and responds well to radiotherapy, may be associated with elevation of which serum marker?","opts":["LDH","AFP","CA-125","Beta-hCG exclusively"],"a":0,"exp":"Serum LDH may be elevated in dysgerminoma and can be used to help monitor the tumor.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Gestational Pathology","q":"Placenta previa, implantation of the placenta over the cervical os, presents with bleeding during which trimester and often requires which mode of delivery?","opts":["Third-trimester bleeding, requiring caesarian section","Second-trimester bleeding, requiring induction","First-trimester bleeding, requiring vaginal delivery","No bleeding, with normal vaginal delivery"],"a":0,"exp":"Because the placenta overlies the cervical os in placenta previa, vaginal delivery risks severe hemorrhage, so caesarian delivery is generally required.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"COPD","q":"In obstructive lung diseases such as COPD, pulmonary function testing shows a decreased FEV1 out of proportion to FVC, resulting in a:","opts":["Increased FEV1:FVC ratio","Decreased FEV1:FVC ratio","Unchanged FEV1:FVC ratio","Decreased total lung capacity"],"a":1,"exp":"Airway obstruction disproportionately reduces FEV1, lowering the FEV1:FVC ratio, while air trapping increases total lung capacity.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Acute Renal Failure","q":"Which type of urinary cast is characteristic of acute tubular necrosis?","opts":["Fatty casts","RBC casts","Brown, granular casts","WBC casts"],"a":2,"exp":"Necrotic tubular cells slough off and form brown, granular casts that plug the tubules.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Acute Renal Failure","q":"A patient in cardiac failure develops decreased renal blood flow, oliguria, a BUN:Cr ratio > 15, and FENa < 1%. This is most consistent with:","opts":["Prerenal azotemia","Acute interstitial nephritis","Acute tubular necrosis","Postrenal azotemia"],"a":0,"exp":"Decreased blood flow with preserved tubular function (low FENa, high urine osmolality, high BUN:Cr) is classic for prerenal azotemia.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Prostate Adenocarcinoma","q":"A higher Gleason score in prostate adenocarcinoma correlates with:","opts":["Lower risk of metastasis","A worse prognosis","A better prognosis","No prognostic significance"],"a":1,"exp":"Higher Gleason scores reflect more disorganized tumor architecture and correlate with more aggressive disease and worse outcomes.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Urinary Tract Infection","q":"The most common causative organism of cystitis is:","opts":["Staphylococcus saprophyticus","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Proteus mirabilis","Escherichia coli"],"a":3,"exp":"E. coli causes approximately 80% of cystitis cases.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Pulmonary Hypertension","q":"Severe, long-standing pulmonary hypertension is characterized pathologically by atherosclerosis of the pulmonary trunk, smooth muscle hypertrophy, intimal fibrosis, and:","opts":["Plexiform lesions","Hyaline membranes","Asteroid bodies","Ghon complexes"],"a":0,"exp":"Plexiform lesions, complex vascular proliferations, are a hallmark of severe, chronic pulmonary hypertension.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Ovarian Tumors \u2013 Germ Cell","q":"Dysgerminoma, the most common malignant germ cell tumor of the ovary, is composed of large cells with clear cytoplasm resembling oocytes, and its testicular counterpart is called:","opts":["Sertoli cell tumor","Seminoma","Yolk sac tumor","Embryonal carcinoma"],"a":1,"exp":"Dysgerminoma and testicular seminoma share identical histology and an excellent response to radiotherapy.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Colorectal Carcinoma","q":"Besides the adenoma-carcinoma sequence, a second important molecular pathway leading to colorectal carcinoma involves:","opts":["Microsatellite instability","VHL gene loss","Chronic H. pylori infection","Chromosomal translocation of WT1"],"a":0,"exp":"Microsatellite instability, reflecting defective DNA mismatch repair, is a second major pathway to colorectal carcinoma.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Cervix","q":"Cervical carcinoma, most commonly seen in middle-aged women, classically presents with:","opts":["Painless postmenopausal bleeding only","Postcoital vaginal bleeding","Amenorrhea","Sudden severe pelvic pain"],"a":1,"exp":"Postcoital bleeding and abnormal cervical discharge are the classic presenting symptoms of cervical carcinoma.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Cervix","q":"High-risk HPV promotes cervical dysplasia through E6 and E7 viral proteins, which increase destruction of p53 and:","opts":["VHL, respectively","BRCA1, respectively","APC, respectively","Rb (retinoblastoma protein), respectively"],"a":3,"exp":"E6 degrades p53 and E7 degrades Rb, inactivating two critical tumor suppressor pathways and promoting cervical dysplasia.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Vagina","q":"Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma (sarcoma botryoides), presenting as a grape-like mass protruding from the vagina in a child under age 5, is composed of immature:","opts":["Skeletal muscle","Cartilage","Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle"],"a":0,"exp":"This rare malignant mesenchymal tumor arises from immature skeletal muscle, characteristically presenting with vaginal bleeding and a grape-like mass in young children.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Diabetes Mellitus","q":"A diagnosis of diabetes mellitus can be made with a fasting glucose level greater than:","opts":["100 mg/dL","126 mg/dL","180 mg/dL","140 mg/dL"],"a":1,"exp":"A fasting plasma glucose above 126 mg/dL meets diagnostic criteria for diabetes mellitus.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Testicular Germ Cell Tumors","q":"Recognized risk factors for testicular germ cell tumors include cryptorchidism and:","opts":["Klinefelter syndrome","Marfan syndrome","Turner syndrome","Down syndrome"],"a":0,"exp":"Klinefelter syndrome and cryptorchidism are both established risk factors for germ cell tumor development.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Thyroid Gland","q":"Myxedema, hypothyroidism in older children and adults, is most commonly caused by iodine deficiency and:","opts":["Hashimoto thyroiditis","Pituitary adenoma","Subacute granulomatous thyroiditis","Graves disease"],"a":0,"exp":"Hashimoto thyroiditis and iodine deficiency are the leading causes of myxedema, with glycosaminoglycan accumulation causing a deepened voice and enlarged tongue.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease","q":"Ulcerative colitis classically presents with left lower quadrant pain and:","opts":["Bilious vomiting","Non-bloody diarrhea","Constipation alternating with bloating","Bloody diarrhea"],"a":3,"exp":"UC symptoms reflect rectal involvement, presenting as LLQ pain with bloody diarrhea.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Endometrium & Myometrium","q":"Growth of the endometrium during the proliferative phase of the menstrual cycle is driven by:","opts":["LH","Prolactin","Progesterone","Estrogen"],"a":3,"exp":"Estrogen drives endometrial proliferation, while progesterone subsequently prepares the endometrium for implantation during the secretory phase.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia","q":"Newborn screening for congenital adrenal hyperplasia is performed by measuring serum levels of:","opts":["ACTH","Aldosterone","17-hydroxyprogesterone","Cortisol"],"a":2,"exp":"17-hydroxyprogesterone, a precursor that accumulates in 21- and 11-hydroxylase deficiency (and is decreased in 17-hydroxylase deficiency), is used for routine newborn CAH screening.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Thyroid Neoplasia","q":"Familial cases of medullary thyroid carcinoma are associated with MEN 2A and 2B, both linked to mutations in:","opts":["The APC gene","The BRCA1 gene","The VHL gene","The RET oncogene"],"a":3,"exp":"RET oncogene mutations underlie MEN 2A and 2B, and their detection warrants consideration of prophylactic thyroidectomy.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Diabetes Mellitus","q":"Hyperosmolar nonketotic coma, a complication of type 2 diabetes with glucose levels often exceeding 500 mg/dL, differs from DKA in that ketones are typically absent because of:","opts":["Small amounts of circulating insulin that prevent significant lipolysis","Coexisting adrenal insufficiency","Rapid renal clearance of ketone bodies","Complete absence of glucagon secretion"],"a":0,"exp":"Residual insulin secretion in type 2 diabetes is enough to suppress significant lipolysis and ketogenesis, even amid severe hyperglycemia and dehydration.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Oral Cavity & Salivary Gland","q":"The major risk factors for oral squamous cell carcinoma, which most commonly arises on the floor of the mouth, are:","opts":["Excess dietary nitrosamines","Chronic candidiasis","Tobacco and alcohol","HPV infection alone"],"a":2,"exp":"Tobacco and alcohol are the major risk factors for oral SCC, which most commonly develops on the floor of the mouth.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Prostate Adenocarcinoma","q":"Localized prostate adenocarcinoma is typically treated with:","opts":["Hormone suppression alone","Chemotherapy alone","Prostatectomy","Radical orchiectomy"],"a":2,"exp":"Surgical prostatectomy is the standard treatment for disease confined to the prostate.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Respiratory Distress Syndromes","q":"ARDS presents with hypoxemia and cyanosis due to a thickened diffusion barrier and alveolar collapse, along with which characteristic chest x-ray finding?","opts":["'White-out' appearance","'Honeycomb' pattern","'Coin lesion'","'Ground-glass' appearance localized to the apex"],"a":0,"exp":"Diffuse alveolar damage and edema in ARDS produce a diffuse 'white-out' appearance on chest x-ray.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Small Bowel","q":"The herpes-like skin vesicles sometimes seen in celiac disease, called dermatitis herpetiformis, result from:","opts":["Autoantibodies against desmoglein","Direct gluten deposition in skin","IgA deposition at the tips of dermal papillae","IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation"],"a":2,"exp":"Dermatitis herpetiformis is caused by IgA deposition at dermal papillae tips and resolves with a gluten-free diet.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Gestational Pathology","q":"The key risk factor for ectopic pregnancy is scarring, most often secondary to pelvic inflammatory disease or:","opts":["Placenta previa","Endometriosis","Preeclampsia","Hydatidiform mole"],"a":1,"exp":"Tubal scarring from PID or endometriosis impairs normal transport of the fertilized ovum, predisposing to ectopic implantation.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Asthma","q":"A severe, unrelenting asthma attack that fails to respond to standard treatment is termed:","opts":["Bronchiectasis","Status asthmaticus","Cor pulmonale","ARDS"],"a":1,"exp":"Status asthmaticus is a life-threatening, prolonged asthma exacerbation that can result in respiratory failure and death if untreated.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Pancreatic Endocrine Neoplasms","q":"An insulinoma presents with episodic hypoglycemia and mental status changes relieved by glucose administration, along with laboratory findings of low serum glucose and:","opts":["Elevated glucagon and low insulin","Elevated gastrin levels","Elevated vasoactive intestinal peptide","Elevated insulin and C-peptide levels"],"a":3,"exp":"Insulinomas autonomously secrete insulin, causing hypoglycemia with elevated insulin and C-peptide, the latter confirming endogenous insulin production.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Ovary Basics & PCOD","q":"In the ovarian follicle, LH acts on theca cells to induce androgen production, while FSH stimulates granulosa cells to:","opts":["Convert androgen to estradiol","Secrete LH","Inhibit androgen synthesis in theca cells","Produce progesterone directly"],"a":0,"exp":"FSH-driven aromatization of theca cell-derived androgen into estradiol by granulosa cells drives the proliferative phase of the endometrial cycle.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease","q":"Crohn disease can affect any part of the GI tract from mouth to anus with skip lesions, most commonly involving the:","opts":["Stomach antrum","Terminal ileum","Rectum","Esophagus"],"a":1,"exp":"The terminal ileum is the most common site of involvement in Crohn disease, while the rectum is least commonly involved.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Stomach","q":"The intestinal type of gastric carcinoma is associated with intestinal metaplasia, nitrosamines, blood type A, and most commonly involves the:","opts":["Pylorus with linitis plastica","Lesser curvature of the antrum","Greater curvature of the fundus","Gastroesophageal junction exclusively"],"a":1,"exp":"Intestinal-type gastric carcinoma resembles a gastric ulcer and typically arises on the lesser curvature of the antrum.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Stomach","q":"Acute gastritis following a severe burn, known as a Curling ulcer, results from:","opts":["Direct thermal injury to the stomach","Increased intracranial pressure stimulating the vagus nerve","H. pylori superinfection","Hypovolemia decreasing mucosal blood supply"],"a":3,"exp":"Curling ulcers occur when burn-induced hypovolemia reduces gastric mucosal blood flow, impairing mucosal defenses.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Renal Neoplasia","q":"The most common histologic subtype of renal cell carcinoma is:","opts":["Collecting duct type","Papillary type","Clear cell type","Chromophobe type"],"a":2,"exp":"Clear cell carcinoma, with abundant clear cytoplasm, is the most common RCC variant.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Testicle Disorders","q":"Mumps orchitis, which carries an increased risk of infertility, is most commonly seen in:","opts":["Prepubertal boys under age 10","Teenage males","Neonates","Elderly men"],"a":1,"exp":"Mumps orchitis typically affects teenage or postpubertal males; testicular inflammation from mumps is uncommon in prepubertal children.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Prostatitis & BPH","q":"In addition to treating BPH, 5\u03b1-reductase inhibitors are also used to treat:","opts":["Erectile dysfunction","Male pattern baldness","Hypogonadism","Testicular torsion"],"a":1,"exp":"By blocking DHT formation, 5\u03b1-reductase inhibitors are also effective for androgenic alopecia (male pattern baldness).","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Adrenal Cortex \u2013 Hyperaldosteronism","q":"Liddle syndrome mimics hyperaldosteronism due to increased activity of sodium channels in the collecting tubule, but is treated with potassium-sparing diuretics such as amiloride rather than spironolactone because:","opts":["Liddle syndrome is caused by excess aldosterone secretion","Spironolactone worsens hyperkalemia in this condition","Aldosterone and renin levels are low, so blocking the aldosterone receptor is ineffective","Amiloride directly increases aldosterone synthase activity"],"a":2,"exp":"Since Liddle syndrome involves a primary sodium channel defect rather than excess aldosterone, spironolactone (which blocks the aldosterone receptor) is ineffective; channel blockers like amiloride work directly.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Endometrium & Myometrium","q":"Acute endometritis, a bacterial infection of the endometrium presenting with fever, abnormal bleeding, and pelvic pain, is usually due to:","opts":["An IUD alone","Retained products of conception","Endometrial hyperplasia","Tuberculosis"],"a":1,"exp":"Retained products of conception after delivery or miscarriage serve as a nidus for bacterial infection in acute endometritis.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Colon Disorders","q":"Angiodysplasia, an acquired malformation of mucosal and submucosal capillaries, most often arises in the cecum and right colon due to:","opts":["Portal hypertension","Low-fiber diet","High wall tension","Chronic infection"],"a":2,"exp":"High wall tension in the cecum and right colon predisposes to angiodysplasia, which classically ruptures causing hematochezia in older adults.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Oral Cavity & Salivary Gland","q":"An aphthous ulcer is classically described as a painful oral lesion with:","opts":["A white plaque that cannot be scraped off","A heaped-up, irregular margin","A vesicular eruption that ruptures","A grayish base surrounded by erythema"],"a":3,"exp":"Aphthous ulcers show a grayish base with surrounding erythema; they arise with stress and resolve spontaneously but often recur.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Testicle Disorders","q":"In older adults, orchitis is most often caused by organisms that ascend from urinary tract infections, namely:","opts":["Escherichia coli and Pseudomonas","Treponema pallidum","Mumps virus","Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae"],"a":0,"exp":"In older men, UTI pathogens like E. coli and Pseudomonas commonly spread to cause orchitis, in contrast to the sexually transmitted causes seen in young adults.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Posterior Pituitary","q":"SIADH leads to retention of free water, resulting in hyponatremia and risk of seizures due to:","opts":["Hypocalcemic tetany","Cerebral vasospasm","Osmotic demyelination from rapid correction","Cerebral edema from neuronal swelling"],"a":3,"exp":"Excess free water retention in SIADH causes hyponatremia, leading to neuronal swelling and cerebral edema, which can cause seizures and mental status changes.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Congenital","q":"Which congenital renal anomaly is the most common overall?","opts":["Dysplastic kidney","Horseshoe kidney","Renal agenesis","Medullary cystic kidney disease"],"a":1,"exp":"Horseshoe kidney, conjoined kidneys usually fused at the lower pole, is the most common congenital renal anomaly.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Thyroid Gland","q":"Graves disease, the most common cause of hyperthyroidism, is caused by an IgG autoantibody that:","opts":["Destroys thyroid follicular cells","Binds and neutralizes circulating T4","Stimulates the TSH receptor","Blocks the TSH receptor"],"a":2,"exp":"Graves disease results from a type II hypersensitivity reaction in which stimulating autoantibodies activate the TSH receptor, increasing thyroid hormone synthesis.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Prostate Adenocarcinoma","q":"A PSA level greater than which value is considered highly worrisome for prostate cancer at any age?","opts":["4 ng/mL","50 ng/mL","2 ng/mL","10 ng/mL"],"a":3,"exp":"A PSA above 10 ng/mL is concerning for malignancy regardless of the patient's age.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Thyroid Gland","q":"Laboratory findings in Graves disease characteristically show elevated free T4 and:","opts":["Elevated TRH","Normal TSH","Decreased TSH","Elevated TSH"],"a":2,"exp":"Excess thyroid hormone in Graves disease suppresses TSH via negative feedback, so TSH is low while free T4 is elevated.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Endometrium & Myometrium","q":"Involvement of the uterine myometrium by endometrial glands and stroma, distinct from endometriosis at other sites, is termed:","opts":["Adenosis","Adenomyosis","Leiomyomatosis","Asherman syndrome"],"a":1,"exp":"When ectopic endometrial tissue infiltrates the myometrium itself, this specific form of endometriosis is called adenomyosis.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Nephrolithiasis","q":"The classic presentation of nephrolithiasis includes colicky pain with hematuria and:","opts":["Bilateral flank tenderness","Periorbital edema","Painless gross hematuria only","Unilateral flank tenderness"],"a":3,"exp":"Nephrolithiasis presents with colicky pain, hematuria, and unilateral flank tenderness.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Diabetes Mellitus","q":"Osmotic damage in diabetes occurs when aldose reductase converts intracellular glucose to sorbitol within Schwann cells, retinal pericytes, and the lens, contributing to peripheral neuropathy, cataracts, and:","opts":["Ageusia","Anosmia","Blindness","Deafness"],"a":2,"exp":"Sorbitol accumulation causes osmotic cellular damage; diabetes is the leading cause of blindness in the developed world through this and vascular mechanisms.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Pancreatic Endocrine Neoplasms","q":"A gastrinoma causes Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, presenting with treatment-resistant peptic ulcers that may extend into the:","opts":["Esophagus","Sigmoid colon","Jejunum","Gallbladder"],"a":2,"exp":"Excess gastrin from a gastrinoma drives severe acid hypersecretion, producing multiple ulcers that can extend beyond the duodenum into the jejunum.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Pulmonary Hypertension","q":"Primary pulmonary hypertension, classically seen in young adult females, is associated in familial cases with inactivating mutations of:","opts":["CFTR","APC","BMPR2","RET"],"a":2,"exp":"BMPR2 mutations lead to unchecked proliferation of pulmonary vascular smooth muscle, contributing to familial primary pulmonary hypertension.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Nephrotic Syndrome","q":"Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in which population?","opts":["Elderly patients with diabetes","Hispanics and African Americans","Children under age 5","Caucasian adults"],"a":1,"exp":"FSGS is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in Hispanic and African American patients.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Colonic Polyps & Polyposis Syndromes","q":"Without prophylactic colectomy, patients with familial adenomatous polyposis almost universally develop colorectal carcinoma by what age?","opts":["20 years","80 years","40 years","60 years"],"a":2,"exp":"Nearly all untreated FAP patients develop colorectal carcinoma by age 40, prompting prophylactic colon and rectal removal.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Colorectal Carcinoma","q":"CEA is a serum tumor marker used in colorectal carcinoma primarily to:","opts":["Monitor treatment response and detect recurrence","Diagnose the initial tumor","Screen asymptomatic average-risk patients","Determine tumor grade"],"a":0,"exp":"CEA is useful for following treatment response and detecting recurrence but is not sensitive or specific enough for screening.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Lung Cancer","q":"An apical lung tumor (Pancoast tumor) involving the sympathetic chain and brachial plexus can cause Horner syndrome, characterized by ptosis, miosis, and:","opts":["Hyperhidrosis","Exophthalmos","Anhidrosis","Mydriasis"],"a":2,"exp":"Pancoast tumors compress the sympathetic chain, producing Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis) along with brachial plexus involvement causing shoulder pain and hand weakness.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Pheochromocytoma","q":"Prior to surgical resection of a pheochromocytoma, patients are treated first with phenoxybenzamine and then with a beta-blocker in order to:","opts":["Prevent postoperative adrenal insufficiency","Prevent a hypertensive crisis from catecholamine release during tumor manipulation","Reduce the risk of thyroid storm","Shrink the tumor before surgery"],"a":1,"exp":"Alpha-blockade with phenoxybenzamine (followed by beta-blockade) prevents a hypertensive crisis when catecholamines are released during tumor handling.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Ovarian Tumors \u2013 Germ Cell","q":"Germ cell tumors, the second most common type of ovarian tumor, usually occur in women of which age group?","opts":["Neonates","Postmenopausal women","Prepubertal children exclusively","Reproductive age"],"a":3,"exp":"Unlike surface epithelial tumors, which favor older women, ovarian germ cell tumors typically occur in women of reproductive age.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Urinary Tract Infection","q":"A diagnosis of cystitis by urinalysis typically requires greater than how many WBCs per high power field?","opts":["5","50","2","10"],"a":3,"exp":"Cystitis is associated with cloudy urine and greater than 10 WBCs per high power field.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Cervix","q":"The junction between the exocervix and endocervix, the site most susceptible to HPV infection, is called the:","opts":["Fornix","Internal os","Transformation zone","Squamocolumnar junction of the vagina"],"a":2,"exp":"HPV preferentially infects the transformation zone, the border between squamous exocervical and columnar endocervical epithelium.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Bronchiectasis","q":"Long-standing bronchiectasis can be complicated by cor pulmonale and secondary systemic amyloidosis of which type?","opts":["AL amyloidosis","Transthyretin amyloidosis","Beta-2 microglobulin amyloidosis","AA amyloidosis"],"a":3,"exp":"Chronic inflammatory states like bronchiectasis can drive secondary (AA-type) amyloid deposition from prolonged serum amyloid A production.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Endometrium & Myometrium","q":"An endometrial polyp, a hyperplastic protrusion of endometrium presenting with abnormal uterine bleeding, can arise as a side effect of:","opts":["Metformin","Tamoxifen","Levothyroxine","Spironolactone"],"a":1,"exp":"Tamoxifen has anti-estrogenic effects on breast tissue but weak pro-estrogenic effects on the endometrium, predisposing to polyp formation.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Esophagus","q":"Barrett esophagus involves metaplasia of the esophageal mucosa from stratified squamous epithelium to:","opts":["Simple squamous epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Nonciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells","Ciliated pseudostratified epithelium"],"a":2,"exp":"Barrett esophagus is a response to chronic acid injury, converting squamous epithelium to nonciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells, which may progress to dysplasia and adenocarcinoma.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Prostate Adenocarcinoma","q":"Prostate adenocarcinoma commonly metastasizes to the lumbar spine and pelvis, producing which type of bone lesion?","opts":["Osteolytic metastases","Osteoblastic metastases","No radiographic bone changes","Mixed lytic-blastic lesions only in the skull"],"a":1,"exp":"Unlike most cancers that cause lytic bone lesions, prostate cancer characteristically produces osteoblastic (bone-forming) metastases.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"},{"cat":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease","q":"A complication linked to disease extent and duration in ulcerative colitis, generally not a concern until more than 10 years of disease, is:","opts":["Fistula formation","Ankylosing spondylitis","Toxic megacolon and carcinoma","Calcium oxalate nephrolithiasis"],"a":2,"exp":"Long-standing extensive ulcerative colitis raises the risk of toxic megacolon and colorectal carcinoma; fistulas and nephrolithiasis are more typical of Crohn disease.","chapter":"Gastrointestinal Pathology"},{"cat":"Adrenal Insufficiency","q":"Acute adrenal insufficiency can result from abrupt glucocorticoid withdrawal or from hemorrhagic necrosis of the adrenal glands, known as:","opts":["Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome","Nelson syndrome","Sheehan syndrome","Conn syndrome"],"a":0,"exp":"Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome is adrenal hemorrhagic necrosis classically associated with Neisseria meningitidis sepsis and DIC in children.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Pulmonary Infections","q":"Klebsiella pneumoniae classically produces thick, gelatinous 'currant jelly' sputum and typically affects:","opts":["Patients with cystic fibrosis exclusively","Young, healthy military recruits","Farmers and veterinarians","Malnourished and debilitated individuals, including alcoholics and diabetics"],"a":3,"exp":"Klebsiella's thick mucoid capsule produces distinctive currant-jelly sputum, with infection classically occurring in debilitated or elderly patients, alcoholics, and diabetics.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Nephrolithiasis","q":"The most common composition of kidney stones overall is:","opts":["Calcium oxalate and/or calcium phosphate","Cystine","Uric acid","Ammonium magnesium phosphate"],"a":0,"exp":"Calcium oxalate and/or calcium phosphate stones are the most common type of kidney stone.","chapter":"Kidney & Urinary Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Nasopharynx & Larynx","q":"Aspirin-intolerant asthma is characterized by the triad of asthma, aspirin-induced bronchospasm, and:","opts":["Eczema","Allergic rhinitis","Charcot-Leyden crystals","Nasal polyps"],"a":3,"exp":"This triad, seen in about 10% of asthmatic adults, links aspirin sensitivity to bronchospasm and nasal polyp formation.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Gestational Pathology","q":"Spontaneous abortion, occurring before 20 weeks gestation in up to a quarter of recognizable pregnancies, is most often due to:","opts":["Preeclampsia","Gestational diabetes","Placenta previa","Chromosomal anomalies, especially trisomy 16"],"a":3,"exp":"Chromosomal abnormalities, most commonly trisomy 16, are the leading cause of first-trimester spontaneous abortion.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Asthma","q":"In the early-phase reaction of an asthma attack, reexposure to allergen triggers IgE-mediated mast cell activation, releasing preformed histamine and generating which mediators that cause bronchoconstriction?","opts":["Complement C3a and C5a","Leukotrienes C4, D4, and E4","Interferon-gamma","Prostaglandin E2 alone"],"a":1,"exp":"Mast cell degranulation releases histamine and generates leukotrienes C4, D4, and E4, which drive bronchoconstriction, inflammation, and edema.","chapter":"Respiratory Tract Pathology"},{"cat":"Thyroid Neoplasia","q":"Follicular carcinoma is distinguished from follicular adenoma by the presence of:","opts":["Psammoma bodies","Amyloid deposition","Invasion through the tumor capsule","Nuclear grooves"],"a":2,"exp":"Capsular invasion differentiates follicular carcinoma from adenoma; because FNA cannot assess the capsule, this distinction requires full histologic examination.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Cushing Syndrome","q":"The central fat redistribution seen in Cushing syndrome, producing moon facies, buffalo hump, and truncal obesity, occurs because high cortisol leads to:","opts":["High insulin levels that increase central fat storage","Direct lipolysis in central fat depots","Decreased catecholamine sensitivity","Increased growth hormone secretion"],"a":0,"exp":"Cortisol-driven hyperglycemia raises insulin levels, which promotes central fat storage, producing the classic Cushingoid body habitus.","chapter":"Endocrine Pathology"},{"cat":"Ovarian Tumors \u2013 Sex Cord-Stromal & Metastasis","q":"Granulosa-theca cell tumor, a neoplastic proliferation that often produces estrogen, most commonly presents in postmenopausal women as:","opts":["Precocious puberty","Endometrial hyperplasia with postmenopausal uterine bleeding","Ascites and pleural effusion","Hirsutism and virilization"],"a":1,"exp":"Estrogen excess from a granulosa-theca cell tumor in postmenopausal women drives endometrial hyperplasia and abnormal bleeding, though the tumor can also cause precocious puberty or menstrual irregularities depending on age.","chapter":"Female Genital System & Gestational Pathology"},{"cat":"Sex Cord-Stromal Tumors & Lymphoma","q":"Sex cord-stromal tumors of the testicle, which resemble normal sex cord-stromal tissue, are usually:","opts":["Associated with elevated AFP","Malignant","Benign","Fatal without treatment"],"a":2,"exp":"In contrast to germ cell tumors, sex cord-stromal tumors of the testis are usually benign.","chapter":"Male Genital System Pathology"}];

